          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"tolstoy-war-and-peace-4fe2d8d695be8d2e\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Pierre’s “Я никогда не перестаю тебя любить” (“I never stop loving you”) directly avows the uninterrupted persistence of his love. Its core force is truth-conditional: he distinguishes his constant love from the especially renewed feeling caused by separation. The wording does not itself undertake a new commitment, and its reflective, corrective delivery supplies no independent expressive/reflexive force.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The marital setting and reassuring effect create slight pragmatic pressure toward P, but the core wording presents continuity of feeling rather than making or renewing an undertaking.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Pierre directly addresses his wife Natasha in an intimate conversation after his return from a separation.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Pierre responds to Natasha’s sudden declaration of love by explaining that separation has given him a special feeling toward her. He corrects her apparent interpretation—“No, not that”—and says his love itself never ceases and cannot increase further; what is new is the post-separation quality he cannot finish articulating. Their exchanged glance completes the thought. This reflective contrast strongly supports an avowal of an enduring state rather than a promise or reflex-like discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Я никогда не перестаю тебя любить” (“I never stop loving you”).\",\"supports\":\"The continuative present-tense formulation explicitly presents Pierre’s enduring loving state as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Pierre prefaces the statement with “Нет не то” (“No, not that”) and follows it with “И больше любить нельзя; а это особенно...” (“And it is impossible to love you more; but this is something special...”).\",\"supports\":\"His deliberate correction distinguishes constant love from a special post-separation feeling, indicating reflective avowal rather than a new undertaking or reflexive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Он не договорил, потому что встретившийся взгляд их договорил остальное” (“He did not finish, because their meeting eyes said the rest”).\",\"supports\":\"The unfinished explanation and shared gaze add intimacy and contextual emotional intensity, but do not alter the core love clause into P, E, or O.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is identified as an affirmative, present-tense canonical love expression in Tolstoy’s Война и мир.\",\"supports\":\"The supplied linguistic metadata agrees with treating the highlighted clause as a direct affirmative presentation of continuing love.\",\"confidence\":0.97}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal. The marital and emotional context can be described separately without requiring P, E, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
      ],
